{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791596088,"nonce":"WUU3T+s0J2Arf2HK1qNSW8QbX90=","message":"Flares joined with a plain min (2026-10-10), from the other SDK's finding: the faint row above a concave-corner box's top edge is smin bulging the edge a flare shares with the body, and liquid glass, which refracts along the distance gradient, shows it as a bright sliver at the flare's inner end. In sdNotch the four flare joins are now std.min; bulge and peak keep their smin, since they round their feet.\n\nMeasured here against the original shader: a dropdown cell differs in 44 pixels (32 by more than 24 levels), with a border 74, with an outer shadow 94, with a 4px inset ring 92; cells without a concave corner are unchanged. A glass concave-top notch over a gradient loses the hotspot at the flare's inner end.\n\nContrary to the report, the flare's reach into the body still matters: with a plain min and an uncapped flare rectangle, fills and borders are identical (0 pixels) but a glass notch gets a yellow wedge just inside each flare's inner end (60 pixels, up to 152 levels), because the rectangle's far edge sits inside the body and shows in the gradient. So flares keep a reach of min(8, into) (flareInto) and bulge and peak reach all the way. No test pins the glass render; the notch test pins fills, borders, outer and inset shadows and the wide-ring seams.","files":null}]}